    43	# Codex Adversarial Review
    44	
    45	Target: working tree diff
    46	Verdict: needs-attention
    47	
    48	Do not ship this as an executable research proposal. The primary endpoint is undefined for the motivating failure, the final test cohort influences search, and the predictor and cold-start comparisons cannot support their headline claims as specified.
    49	
    50	Findings:
    51	- [high] The primary endpoint is undefined for the motivating failure (docs/idea-summaries/05-what-makes-a-harness-improvable.md:112-116)
    52	  The pilot reports 162 missing scores out of 729 and zero Y-Balance scores. The profile requires every child to return a finite score on every input, then divides by the parent's selection MAE. For a parent that never scores Y-Balance, that denominator is undefined unless the evaluator supplies a missing-score rule. If MAE is computed over successful predictions, restoring difficult trials can make an actual repair look worse, and candidates can be compared on different people.
    53	  Recommendation: Freeze the eligible trials and specify a loss for missing, invalid, or timed-out predictions on every split. Use the same denominators for parents and children, report coverage separately, and define the gain when parent loss is zero.
    54	- [high] The final test participants influence which refactors survive (docs/idea-summaries/05-what-makes-a-harness-improvable.md:104-106)
    55	  The gate executes candidates on test participants before admitting them. H6 reuses that gate to accept each proposed refactor at line 152, so even a single pass/fail bit steers the search using the final cohort's inputs. H6 also gives this access only to gated arms. The participant-split figure says that search never reads those people, and the same cohort already supplied the pilot findings that motivate the design.
    56	  Recommendation: Run all adaptive gates on development and synthetic cases, or use a separate equivalence cohort. Freeze all artifacts before an isolated final audit and use a fresh participant cohort for the confirmatory claim.
    57	- [high] H3 leaves the forecast undefined and can reward between-base differences (docs/idea-summaries/05-what-makes-a-harness-improvable.md:74-82)
    58	  H3 specifies one Pearson correlation for a four-component profile without defining how the components become a scalar prediction or where that mapping is fit. The 96 configurations are 32 variants of each of three bases, all scored on the same 43 test people. A pooled correlation can clear 0.6 by ranking the three bases by headroom while failing to rank their variants. The stated power and Williams test also lack a treatment of this dependence and of the proposed attenuation correction.
    59	  Recommendation: Freeze a scalar forecast before opening test outcomes. Make incremental within-base prediction the primary comparison and use paired inference that preserves the base, run, and shared-participant structure. Add held-out bases if claiming prediction on new harnesses.
    60	- [high] The cold-start comparison substitutes a weaker rule for HGM (docs/idea-summaries/05-what-makes-a-harness-improvable.md:138-148)
    61	  Published HGM pools passed and failed benchmark tasks across the clade, including the node's own evaluations. A node with zero descendants can therefore have an informative posterior once it has been scored. Here the prior is fit to whether whole child edits improve by at least 1%, a different event, with no mapping to HGM's counts. Under the proposed flat, descendant-only convention, baseline Pearson correlation at zero descendants is undefined. Figure 6 presents the structural prior as informative before H5 has tested it. [HGM, Sections 3.2–3.3](https://arxiv.org/html/2510.21614#S3.SS3)
    62	  Recommendation: Reproduce HGM's actual update or label the continuous-loss adaptation explicitly. Use the same outcome and observation unit for both priors, distinguish unevaluated nodes from evaluated leaves, and compare prediction error or ranking utility at cold start. Present Figure 6 as a hypothesis.
    63	- [high] H2's power calculation uses four times the sample size of its actual contrast (docs/idea-summaries/05-what-makes-a-harness-improvable.md:223-227)
    64	  The claimed 90% half-width of 0.017 uses 2,400 edits per factor level. H2 compares cells 8 and 1 for one proposer, with 600 edits per cell. At the stated 0.10 rate and design effect of 1.45, the corresponding half-width is about 0.034 without an additional pairing benefit, wider than the ±0.025 equivalence margin. The proposal supplies no estimate of the pairing benefit. All full loops also use the 30B proposer, so the design can only test the crossover for one-step edits.
    65	  Recommendation: Power the actual paired cell-by-proposer contrast under plausible covariance and base heterogeneity. Increase direct replication or label H2 exploratory, and include stronger proposers in the loop study if the claim concerns long-run gain.
    66	- [high] The break-even model uses a different success event from the search it forecasts (docs/idea-summaries/05-what-makes-a-harness-improvable.md:122-132)
    67	  The multiplier sg counts edits that lower selection MAE by at least 1%. The Meta-Harness-style loop accepts any development improvement, while OpenEvolve can expand an archive node outside the current best lineage. Neither protocol implements the multiplicative incumbent process in the equation. H6 then checks a crossover on selection people, whose labels also define the fitted rates. Agreement could reflect shared cohort preferences while failing on new people.
    68	  Recommendation: Test the formula first with a loop whose acceptance rule matches the profiled event. Estimate the rates before the validation runs, evaluate frozen budget checkpoints on independent participants, and define how to handle no-crossing and multiple-crossing outcomes.
    69	- [medium] The observability arm repeats a known feedback intervention (docs/idea-summaries/05-what-makes-a-harness-improvable.md:90-98)
    70	  O changes the evidence sent to the proposer, T adds diagnostic content to that same stream, and M and P introduce units and semantic hints. Equal token counts leave those differences intact, so a contrast can estimate the effect of the specified package while Figure 1 attributes it to structure alone. Meta-Harness already ablated proposer trace access over a full search. The narrower novelty is whether changing the harness implementation adds value when diagnostic evidence is held fixed, or whether a particular instrumentation policy improves search on this task. [Meta-Harness, Table 3](https://arxiv.org/html/2603.28052#S4.T3)
    71	  Recommendation: Cross code form with trace visibility using an external observer that can supply identical diagnostics to both forms. Describe the remaining contrasts as effects of the specified transformations and revise the novelty claim and Figure 1 accordingly.
    72	- [medium] The compute budget omits work required for selection and refactor validation (docs/idea-summaries/05-what-makes-a-harness-improvable.md:249-268)
    73	  The tier-2 row prices development evaluations, but selection people choose each loop's final candidate. Rescoring all 96,000 candidates on 150 selection examples at the stated throughput would add about 6,000 CPU-hours. A frozen shortlist could reduce that cost, but the proposal specifies none. H6 also has no budget line for its pre- and post-refactor profiles or fuzz gates. Profiling all 120 independently refactored gated runs would require 36,000 post-refactor edits alone.
    74	  Recommendation: Specify a development-only shortlist and the number of distinct refactors to profile. Benchmark and price a complete run, including selection scoring, equivalence checks, predictor measurement, and sealed scoring.
    75	
    76	Next steps:
    77	- Freeze the scoring policy and cohort access rules before generating variants.
    78	- Preregister the predictor, parent-selection baselines, and refactor crossover test, then verify their power and cost in a small end-to-end pilot.

 succeeded in 0ms:
     1	# What makes an agent harness easier to improve?
     2	
     3	> **Research question.** When two harnesses return identical outputs on every input, does the way their code is written change how far a self-improvement loop improves them at a fixed budget, and can a cheap one-step measurement predict it?
     4	>
     5	> **Why it matters.** Self-improving systems pick parents by score or descendant record, and harness-evolution systems assume, untested, that modular, logged code is easier to improve. If so, loops should restructure before searching and rank newborn candidates by code.
     6	>
     7	> **ML contribution.** The decisive experiment rewrites 20 balance-scoring harnesses without changing outputs, measures one-step edits from three LLM proposers across five code properties, and checks them against 960 loops. It yields causal effects, a one-step forecast of loop gain, a code-based parent prior and a break-even budget for refactoring.
     8	>
     9	> **What would prove it wrong.** At matched length, neither structure's effect on one-step success (H1) nor observability's on the loops' sealed-test gain (H1b) reaches the smallest effect of interest, or one-step profiles forecast loop gain no better than descendant counts at equal evaluations (H3).
    10	
    11	## The problem, in one concrete case
    12	
    13	StaBLE [1](#ref-1) is a battery of 18 balance tasks recorded in 180 people with three smartphones and reconstructed with OpenCap, a markerless motion-capture system that estimates movement from two or more phone videos [2](#ref-2). Each task score averages min-max normalized features such as reach distance, extracted by public code [3](#ref-3). A lab pilot (an internal, unpublished account) applied the same rules to single-phone reconstructions from OpenCap Monocular [4](#ref-4). On 43 held-out people its task-averaged mean absolute error (MAE) was 0.146, worse than the 0.120 of a constant guess at each task's training mean. Only 567 of 729 task scores were valid, and Y-Balance, which alone explains 63% of the variance of the overall StaBLE score [1](#ref-1), got none. The reference script finds the reach window from a reversal in toe velocity, sweeping the threshold from 0.7 to 0.3 m/s, and no threshold found a window.
    14	
    15	Now hand this harness to a self-improvement loop, in which a proposer LLM edits the code and better candidates are kept. Version A is one 400-line function with constants inline, so the loop's only evidence about Y-Balance is a missing score. Version S computes the same function in typed stages: pose input (2D keypoints from RTMPose [5](#ref-5) or OpenCap Monocular kinematics), filtering, center of mass, reach window and score. It logs each stage's summary to a trace file, keeps `threshold_mps` and `cutoff_hz` in a configuration file with units, and warns when a reach window is empty (Figure 2). Both return bit-identical scores, yet only S's trace shows that every monocular window was empty and where the toe speeds fell relative to the sweep. Errors in such a harness are silent (a doubled filter cutoff gives plausible scores with larger error), so code form may determine how much evidence a loop can use (Figure 1). If so, can a cheap early measurement predict the difference, and can a loop raise its own improvability by restructuring first?
    16	
    17	Coding agents raise the same question. The Darwin Goedel Machine (DGM) evolves agents that rewrite their own Python code; it raised the Aider Polyglot pass rate from 14.2% to 30.7% along paths that often crossed lower-scoring nodes [6](#ref-6). Work on the Huxley-Goedel Machine (HGM) found that an agent's own score predicts poorly how much its descendants improve [7](#ref-7). Neither asks which properties of an agent's code make it a productive ancestor. Unit tests print failures while a wrong balance score looks like any other number, so if structure helps through the evidence it exposes, logging should help more in the measurement harness (H7).
    18	
    19	![Two harnesses with identical outputs can improve at different rates under the same optimizer and budget](images/05/same-outputs-different-futures.svg)
    20	
    21	*Figure 1. A and S return the same score on every input, so any gap between their curves comes from code form, including the trace it writes. A point is the expected sealed-test improvement after that many labelled evaluations; the curves are illustrative.*
    22	
    23	![The same scoring computation written as one function and as typed stages that write a trace file](images/05/pipeline-two-forms.svg)
    24	
    25	*Figure 2. S computes A's Y-Balance score in five typed stages with configured thresholds and two warning-only checks, and returns a per-stage trace with the score error.*
    26	
    27	## Key ideas in plain terms
    28	
    29	**Harness.** The code around a model or tool that turns inputs into outputs: here, the program mapping a recording's cached pose and kinematics to a task score; in a coding agent, its prompts, tools and control loop.
    30	
    31	**Trace.** A text record a harness writes while it runs, beyond its outputs, such as per-stage keypoint-confidence quantiles or whether a reach window was found.
    32	
    33	**Self-improvement loop and proposer.** A loop repeatedly proposes, evaluates and keeps changes to a harness; it is recursive when the changed code includes the procedure making the changes, as in the Self-Improving Coding Agent (SICA) [8](#ref-8), DGM and HGM. The proposer is the LLM that turns a parent's code, scores and trace into an edit. It acts as a mutation operator conditioned on the code's text, so changing only names, comments or layout can change its edits.
    34	
    35	**Behavior-preserving transformation (BPT).** A rewrite that leaves every output unchanged, such as splitting a function into stages, adding a log line, moving a literal into a configuration file or renaming identifiers (`v` becomes `toe_speed_mps`). Differential testing checks equivalence by running both programs on the same inputs and comparing outputs.
    36	
    37	**Improvability.** Let $J_{dev}$, $J_{sel}$ and $J_{test}$ be a harness's task-averaged MAE on the development, selection and sealed people defined below, with $J=J_{test}$. An optimizer (proposer plus search loop) run from harness $h$ with $B$ labelled candidate evaluations and seed $\omega$ returns a candidate $\hat h_B(h,\omega)$. The improvability of $h$ is
    38	
    39	$$E_B(h)=\mathbb{E}_\omega\left[J(h)-J\big(\hat h_B(h,\omega)\big)\right].$$
    40	
    41	Harnesses computing the same function have equal $J$, so any difference in their improvability comes from code form. Biologists call the analogous property evolvability and expect modular, low-pleiotropy maps (one change affecting few traits) to adapt more readily [9](#ref-9), [10](#ref-10).
    42	
    43	**Metaproductivity and the clade estimate.** Metaproductivity, HGM's term for the same idea, is how much a candidate's descendants improve. HGM estimates it for agent $a$ by pooling its clade (the agent and all its descendants) into $n_s$ successes and $n_f$ failures, a success being a solved benchmark task, and taking $n_s/(n_s+n_f)$ [7](#ref-7), so 3 successes and 1 failure give 0.75. Thompson sampling then draws a value per node from a Beta distribution with these counts and expands the largest draw. A newborn node has only its own evaluations, so its estimate is broad.
    44	
    45	**Success events and the profile.** A *selection success* is a valid edit that lowers $J_{sel}$ by at least $\delta=1\%$ of the parent's value and passes a guard against shrinkage toward task means. A *development success* is the same event with gain and guard computed on development people. Loops accept only these, so the loop model and both forecasts use it, and HGM's counts and the structural prior count it task by task. The *single-edit success profile* summarizes many one-step edits from one parent and proposer (valid rate, success rate, mean gain, regression share); its selection success rate $s$ is the primary outcome.
    46	
    47	**Equivalence test.** Two one-sided tests (TOST) check that an effect lies inside a pre-registered margin, so a null can carry information: with margin 0.025, two success rates are equivalent only if the 90% interval of their difference lies within 0.025 of zero.
    48	
    49	**The design in brief.** A *base* is one independently written harness for one StaBLE task family; there are 20. A *variant* rewrites a base, without changing any output, along five factors with a degraded and a structured level: modularity (M), observability (O), named parameters (P), warning-only checks (T) and naming (N, which also has a generic middle level). A *cell* is one combination of M, O, P and T levels in an eight-cell half fraction; cell 1 has all four degraded and cell 8 all four structured. *Development* people supply errors and traces to the proposer, *selection* people define edit success and pick each loop's final candidate, and *sealed* people, read by no proposer, search or selection step, score each loop's final candidate once. *Tier 1* profiles every cell, base and proposer (30B and 480B open models and a frontier API model). *Tier 2* runs loops with the 30B proposer on 96 *configurations*: three bases times all 32 settings of the five factors.
    50	
    51	## What is known and what is missing
    52	
    53	Harness-evolution systems such as Agentic Harness Engineering (AHE) [11](#ref-11) build on decomposition and observability without removing either, and evidence that code form matters to LLMs is mixed.
    54	
    55	| Work | What it showed | What it leaves open here |
    56	|---|---|---|
    57	| DGM [6](#ref-6) | Self-rewriting coding agents whose lineages pass through lower-scoring nodes | Why some low scorers are good ancestors |
    58	| HGM [7](#ref-7) | Clade estimate of metaproductivity, with Thompson sampling | No code features, so little data at birth |
    59	| AHE [11](#ref-11) | Evolving a decomposed, observable harness lifts the first-attempt pass rate on Terminal-Bench 2 from 69.7% to 77.0% | Ablations add single evolved components; none removes the decomposition or an observability pillar |
    60	| Code for Machines [12](#ref-12) | Medium open models break healthier code less when refactoring (Qwen: 19.3% against 27.8%); Sonnet 4.5's 13.2% against 16.0% is not significant | Observational; no optimizer or budget |
    61	| Predict Before You Modify [13](#ref-13) | Forecasting a diff's outcome before evaluation gives up to 46% higher average performance than HyperAgents | No structural features |
    62	| Meta-Harness [14](#ref-14) | A proposer reading prior code, scores and raw traces beats hand-built harnesses; scores alone or with summaries trail raw traces (median 34.6 and 34.9 against 50.0) | Never varies how the trace-writing harness is coded |
    63	| When Names Disappear [15](#ref-15); Evaluating AGENTS.md [16](#ref-16) | Names change LLM outputs; context files do not raise success | No improvement loop |
    64	
    65	No study we found randomizes behavior-preserving rewrites of a harness and measures how far an LLM optimizer then improves it at a fixed budget, and budget-allocation work ignores restructuring [17](#ref-17). Missing are a causal estimate of each structural property's effect with behavior and length fixed, its dependence on proposer capability, and a cheap predictor of it. Also untested is whether structure can rank newborn parents, where HGM has almost no data, or justify a refactoring phase with no immediate gain, which Predict Before You Modify leaves open [13](#ref-13). Since Meta-Harness shows that what the proposer reads matters, the O question is narrower: does it matter that the harness's own code fills a trace channel of fixed format and length?
    66	
    67	## Hypotheses
    68	
    69	1. **H1, structure raises one-step success.** For the 30B proposer at matched length, cell 8's selection success rate exceeds cell 1's.
    70	2. **H1b, observability raises loop gain.** In tier-2 loops, O raises the final candidate's sealed-test MAE reduction.
    71	3. **H2, the effect shrinks with capability.** The composite effect (cell 8 minus cell 1) declines from the 30B to the 480B to the frontier proposer. For the frontier proposer each M, O, P and T main effect lies within 0.025 of zero, a sharper test of Code for Machines' non-significant Sonnet 4.5 difference [12](#ref-12).
    72	4. **H3, profiles forecast loops.** Within each base, the forecast $\hat F$ from a 300-edit profile correlates at least 0.6 with the mean sealed-test gain of ten loops (an estimate of $E_{100}$), more strongly than HGM's estimate from the same 300 evaluations.
    73	5. **H4, structured cells get smaller, better-aimed edits.** Their successful edits touch fewer lines and break fewer correct examples, and fewer of their edits are invalid. Under O, edits citing a logged quantity succeed more often, and seeded defects are repaired in fewer evaluations.
    74	6. **H5, a structural prior helps at birth.** At 0 and 5 descendant evaluations, code features raise the clade estimate's rank correlation with later metaproductivity and lower the regret of expanding its top node (that node's shortfall from the best). They do not hurt the correlation at 20.
    75	7. **H6, refactoring first pays off past a predicted budget.** From the as-written and the degraded form, refactoring first overtakes search alone at the break-even budget $B^{\star}$ predicted from development-success profiles.
    76	8. **H7 (exploratory), the O effect depends on the domain.** It is larger in the balance harness, whose errors are silent, than in the coding agent, whose tests print failures.
    77	
    78	The table gives each hypothesis's primary test, which for H2 and H4 has more than one leg; every other test is secondary.
    79	
    80	| | Experiment | Primary measurement and test | Falsified if |
    81	|---|---|---|---|
    82	| H1 | 1 | Cell 8 minus cell 1, 1,200 edits per cell; logistic mixed model | Upper end of the 95% interval below 0.05 |
    83	| H1b | 3 | O main effect on sealed-test gain, 480 against 480 loops; paired mixed model | Upper end of the 95% interval below $0.25\sigma_{loop}$ |
    84	| H2 | 1 | One-sided linear trend of the composite over proposers; TOST on each frontier main effect | Trend flat or rising, or a frontier main effect fails TOST |
    85	| H3 | 3 | Raw within-base Pearson correlation; paired bootstrap of its difference from the clade estimate's | Point estimate below 0.6, or the difference's interval includes zero |
    86	| H4 | 1, 2 | Share of the O effect mediated by lines touched and by regressions; hazard of repair; Holm across the three | Neither share excludes zero and O does not shorten repair |
    87	| H5 | 4 | Spearman correlation and regret on 100 archive nodes | No gain at 0 and 5 descendants, or a loss at 20 |
    88	| H6 | 5 | First checkpoint after which refactor-first stays ahead | Crossover outside the prediction's 95% interval, or none by 200 |
    89	| H7 | 1 and second domain | Domain-by-O interaction on the log-odds scale | Reported as an estimate only |
    90	
    91	## Method
    92	
    93	The gate makes variants, the profile measures them (H1, H2, H4), a loop model turns profiles into forecasts and a break-even budget (H3, H6), and a code-feature prior adapts parent selection (H5).
    94	
    95	### Structural factors
    96	
    97	Every base is rewritten to both levels of each factor, so even a well-written base gets a degraded copy.
    98	
    99	- **M:** one inlined function, or one typed function per stage (dataclasses whose annotations give array shapes and units).
   100	- **O:** outputs and exceptions only, or each stage also writes an aggregated summary of its intermediate values to the trace.
   101	- **P:** constants inline, or a typed configuration with names, units and plausible ranges (`cutoff_hz: 6.0`, range 2 to 12).
   102	- **T:** none, or generic assertions on shapes, units, ranges, monotonic time stamps and non-empty event windows; a violation writes a warning and never changes an output.
   103	- **N:** opaque (`v17`), generic (`threshold`) or physical (`toe_speed_mps`), a level that exists only in a scientific harness.
   104	
   105	Scripted syntax-tree transformations apply M, O, P and T, so they compose, after a person marks stage boundaries and constants once per base (Figure 3). An LLM that sees only the code does the renaming. Comments may not contain results, and a reviewer blind to the data audits 10% of variants for smuggled hints.
   106	
   107	![A base harness is rewritten along five factors, padded to one length and admitted only through the equivalence gate](images/05/bpt-equivalence-gate.svg)
   108	
   109	*Figure 3. Each variant takes one level per factor, is padded to a common token count, and enters only if it reproduces the base's scores and tool calls on cached and fuzzed inputs.*
   110	
   111	### Equivalence gate
   112	
   113	A variant passes three checks against its base:
   114	
   115	1. bit-identical task scores on every cached development and selection input (float64, pinned library build; no labels are read);
   116	2. identical scores on 10,000 fuzzed inputs per stage from the Hypothesis property-based testing library (keypoint arrays with gaps and outliers);
   117	3. an identical sequence of tool calls, which here are reads of cached tool outputs (tool, recording, frame range); loop edits may change them, variants may not.
   118	
   119	For new trace lines and warnings, the gate checks only that writing them never changes a score. The gate never runs on sealed inputs, because the refactor-first phase uses it inside search. After all loops end, one audit replays every frozen variant on them. Before outcomes are examined, an audit reports each base's pass rate, branch coverage and starting $J_{sel}$, with the share charged to unscored trials.
   120	
   121	Every variant of base $b$ is padded with neutral comments at function boundaries to a common length $L_b$, set for each proposer's tokenizer to the longest variant's token count plus 2%. Every arm gets one feedback channel of fixed format and length (the code, per-task development errors and the parent's aggregated development trace, with at most five per-trial excerpts), and O and T change only the trace's content.
   122	
   123	### The single-edit success profile
   124	
   125	For parent $h$ and proposer $q$, draw $n$ one-step edits in sessions of ten (Figure 4), giving children $h_1,\dots,h_n$ with
   126	
   127	$$V_i,\;G_i\in\lbrace 0,1\rbrace,\qquad \Delta_i=\frac{J_{sel}(h)-J_{sel}(h_i)}{J_{sel}(h)},\qquad Z_i=V_iG_i\mathbf{1}\lbrace \Delta_i\ge\delta\rbrace,\quad \delta=0.01.$$
   128	
   129	- **Valid.** $V_i=1$ when the child runs on all development and selection inputs within the time limit, raises no exception and leaves frozen files untouched.
   130	- **Scoring rule.** Error uses a frozen list of eligible examples per split (those with a reference score), with one denominator for every candidate. A missing, non-finite or out-of-range score is charged $\max(y,1-y)$ for reference $y$ in $[0,1]$, the worst error an in-range score could have, so a missing score whose reference is 0.8 costs 0.8. Declining to score then never beats a guess, so repairing the unscored Y-Balance task registers as a gain. Coverage is reported separately, and a parent with zero selection error is not profiled.
   131	- **Shrinkage guard.** Since a constant guess beats the pilot harness, pulling outputs toward task means can lower error without measuring anyone better. The guard sets $G_i=0$ when the mean within-task Spearman correlation with reference scores on selection people falls, counting a task with missing or constant outputs as 0. It also sets $G_i=0$ when some task's outputs become constant or lose over half their standard deviation. Such edits are reported separately.
   132	- **Profile.** $(v,s,g,r)$: the means of $V_i$ and of the success indicator $Z_i$, the mean gain of successes, and their mean share of development examples whose error rises by more than 0.02, a pleiotropy proxy. The same rules on development people give the development variant $(s_{dev},g_{dev})$.
   133	
   134	A session is ten fresh continuations from one proposer context and seed, each shown one-line summaries of earlier edits but never selection outcomes; seeds are shared across a base's cells, so contrasts are paired.
   135	
   136	![Thirty one-step edits from one variant, sorted into invalid, valid without gain and valid with gain, summarized as a profile](images/05/single-edit-profile.svg)
   137	
   138	*Figure 4. Thirty one-step edits of one parent in three sessions of ten, scored on selection people (illustrative). $g$ and $r$ need per-edit gains and per-example errors that the squares do not show.*
   139	
   140	### A loop model and a break-even budget
   141	
   142	Treat a loop as accept-if-better search in which each proposal succeeds independently with probability $s$ and each success removes a fraction $g$ of the remaining error. With $K\sim\mathrm{Binomial}(B,s)$ successes in $B$ proposals, the expected fraction of error removed is
   143	
   144	$$F(B)=1-\mathbb{E}\left[(1-g)^{K}\right]=1-(1-sg)^{B}.$$
   145	
   146	The Meta-Harness-style loop accepts only development successes, so the profile measures its acceptance event and forecasts use $(s_{dev},g_{dev})$, with rates estimated as $(k+0.5)/(n+1)$ for $k$ successes in $n$ edits. H3's forecast is $\hat F=F(100)$. A refactor-first phase of fixed cost $C_R$ evaluation-equivalents (the proposer tokens of one search step) moves the profile to $(s',g')$. It is ahead at budget $B$ when $(1-s'g')^{B-C_R}$ falls below $(1-sg)^{B}$, which gives the break-even budget (Figure 5)
   147	
   148	$$B^{\star}=C_R \frac{\ln(1-s'g')}{\ln(1-s'g')-\ln(1-sg)}\approx C_R \frac{s'g'}{s'g'-sg}.$$
   149	
   150	Raising $sg$ from 0.010 to 0.015 gives $B^{\star}\approx 3C_R$, and if $s'g'\le sg$ the model predicts no crossover. It describes the single-incumbent Meta-Harness-style loop, with OpenEvolve as a secondary check, and it ignores the error floor and the decline of $s$ as easy fixes run out; tier 2 tests whether it still ranks configurations and predicts the crossover.
   151	
   152	![Refactoring first starts behind and overtakes search alone at the break-even budget](images/05/refactor-first-break-even.svg)
   153	
   154	*Figure 5. Illustrative loop-model curves with $sg$ raised from 0.010 to 0.015: refactoring first gains nothing while it spends the refactor cost $C_R$, then overtakes search alone at $B^{\star}\approx 3C_R$.*
   155	
   156	### A structural prior for parent selection
   157	
   158	Let $x_a$ be static features of agent $a$'s code: function count and lengths, logged quantities, named parameters and the share with units, assertions, identifiers that are words or carry unit suffixes, and token length. With $p_a=\sigma(w^\top x_a)$, the estimated clade metaproductivity (CMP) and its Thompson draw replace HGM's flat prior (Figure 6):
   159	
   160	$$\widehat{\mathrm{CMP}}(a)=\frac{n_s+\kappa p_a}{n_s+n_f+\kappa},\qquad \mu_a\sim\mathrm{Beta}\big(\tau(n_s+\kappa p_a),\;\tau(n_f+\kappa(1-p_a))\big),$$
   161	
   162	where $n_s$ and $n_f$ count the clade's per-task development successes and failures, $\sigma$ is the logistic function and $\tau$ is HGM's temperature schedule, kept unchanged. Weights $w$ and pseudo-count $\kappa$ are fit by beta-binomial maximum likelihood, which makes the observed counts most probable when each node's rate is drawn from a Beta distribution centered on $p_a$. With $\kappa=10$ and $p_a=0.2$, one success in 4 task counts gives $(1+2)/(4+10)=0.21$, and HGM's Thompson draw is the case $\kappa=2$, $p_a=0.5$. Before any evaluation the estimate is $p_a$, and it converges to HGM's as descendants accumulate. For a node of base $b$, the prior is fit on per-task development outcomes of the other bases' tier-1 children and of archive nodes from the other two tier-2 bases, so training includes LLM-evolved code; each feature's shift between training and test nodes is reported.
   163	
   164	![Hypothesis: code features separate two newborn nodes that DGM and HGM rank alike](images/05/parent-selection-at-birth.svg)
   165	
   166	*Figure 6. The hypothesis H5 tests: two new nodes with degraded and structured code, one evaluation each and the same outcome. DGM's score and HGM's counts cannot tell them apart; the structural prior separates them from code features alone. Densities are illustrative.*
   167	
   168	### Refactor-first phase
   169	
   170	The proposer receives the transformation catalogue and outputs restructuring edits, each kept only if it passes the gate, so the phase spends tokens and CPU time but no labelled evaluations. It makes the loop recursive, since it restructures the code it will later optimize. Proposer tokens are fixed, so this arm gets $C_R$ fewer labelled evaluations; an unverified-refactor arm skips the gate.
   171	
   172	### Optimizers and proposers
   173	
   174	Two optimizers are fixed across arms. OpenEvolve [18](#ref-18) keeps an island archive (sub-populations that evolve separately and exchange candidates) and edits only between evolve markers, comments that here enclose the whole harness body. A Meta-Harness-style loop [14](#ref-14) shows the proposer the incumbent's code and aggregated trace plus a 5,000-token table of the most recent and best earlier candidates (diff summary and scores), and accepts only development successes. Both use one prompt template, temperature 0.8, top-p 0.95, 4,000 output tokens and a 64,000-token context. The proposers are Qwen3-Coder-30B-A3B-Instruct (3.3B of 30.5B parameters active) [19](#ref-19) served with vLLM [20](#ref-20) on one H100, Qwen3-Coder-480B-A35B-Instruct [21](#ref-21) on an eight-H100 node, and one frontier API model pinned to a dated version at registration.
   175	
   176	```
   177	# Tier 1
   178	for base b in bases:                          # 20 independently written harnesses
   179	    for cell c in cells + naming_arms + control_arms:
   180	        h = pad(apply_bpts(b, c), L_b)
   181	        if not equivalence_gate(h, b): log_and_discard(h); continue
   182	        trace = aggregate(run(h, dev_people))  # fixed format and length
   183	        for q in proposers:                    # 30B, 480B, frontier
   184	            for k in range(sessions(q, c)):    # 3 by default; 6 or 7 where powered up
   185	                for i in range(10):
   186	                    diff = q.propose(h.code, dev_errors(h), trace,
   187	                                     earlier=summaries(b, c, q, k), seed=(b, k, i))
   188	                    child = apply(diff, h)
   189	                    for split in (selection_people, dev_people):   # selection and development events
   190	                        record(split, valid(child), rel_gain(child, split), guard(child, split))
   191	                    record(regressions(child, dev_people), lines_touched(diff),
   192	                           cites_logged_quantity(diff.rationale))
   193	```
   194	
   195	## Experiments
   196	
   197	### Data and splits
   198	
   199	The lab's single-camera StaBLE recordings cover 171 participants and 2,968 usable task examples (lab pilot counts). Before any cache, profile or prior is built, they are split by person into 85 development, 43 selection and 43 sealed people (Figure 7). The sealed people are the pilot's test split; no base author sees per-person outputs on them, and the Y-Balance failure is confirmed on development people before any base is written. Normalization bounds are refit on development people, since the published bounds span all 180 people [1](#ref-1). Loops use at least five seeds, because runs are noisy and order-sensitive [22](#ref-22) and selected candidates inherit a winner's curse [23](#ref-23); intervals are clustered by person.
   200	
   201	![Participants are split into development, selection and sealed test groups before anything is built](images/05/participant-splits.svg)
   202	
   203	*Figure 7. The three person-level splits and what each supplies; only development people inform the proposer and the normalization bounds.*
   204	
   205	### Base harnesses
   206	
   207	Twenty bases cover five StaBLE task families (standing, reaching including Y-Balance, sit-to-stand, single-leg, and jump or gait) with four implementations each: a port of the public `get_features` logic [3](#ref-3), two written by different people from the preprint, and one by an LLM outside the proposer families. Bases differ in behavior and enter as a random effect. Tier 2, the defect probe and the refactor experiment use three bases from different families and authors; three others are reserved for parent selection.
   208	
   209	### Baselines
   210	
   211	DGM's score-based parent selection [6](#ref-6); HGM's clade estimate with Thompson sampling [7](#ref-7); its budget-efficient variant HGM-Dual [24](#ref-24); a forecaster in the style of the Improvement Dynamics Model (IDM) of Predict Before You Modify [13](#ref-13), which predicts the outcomes of 300 proposed edits without evaluating them; uniform selection; search alone; and unverified refactoring.
   212	
   213	### Metrics
   214	
   215	The primary tier-1 outcome is $s$; secondary outcomes are $v$, $g$, $r$, coverage, guard failures, lines touched and the share of rationales citing a logged quantity. For loops the primary outcome is the final candidate's sealed-test MAE reduction, with the area under the best-so-far curve and within-task Spearman correlation secondary. The defect probe reports evaluations to repair, localization (whether the edit touches the defective stage) and diagnosis accuracy.
   216	
   217	### Core experiments
   218	
   219	1. **Tier-1 profiles (H1, H2, H4).** A $2^{4-1}$ fractional factorial (Figure 8) over M, O, P and T with defining relation $I=MOPT$ and N generic. In this resolution IV design a main effect is confounded only with a three-way interaction, and two-way interactions in pairs such as MO with PT; cell 2, for example, has only M and T structured. Each variant gets 30 edits per proposer, except that the 30B proposer gets 60 on cells 1 and 8 (for H1) and the frontier proposer 70 on every cell (for H2), giving 22,000 factorial edits. Opaque and physical naming of cells 1 and 8 add 7,200 and the controls 10,200, for 39,400.
   220	2. **Seeded defects (H4).** Four defects are planted identically in all eight cells of three bases, so defective variants stay mutually equivalent: a doubled filter cutoff, swapped anterior-posterior and medial-lateral axes, 30 frames per second assumed for 60, and centimetres read as metres. This gives 480 repair loops (30B proposer, at most 30 evaluations, five seeds). Each proposer output must begin with a stated cause, scored against the defect by an LLM judge outside the proposer set and checked on 10% by two blind raters.
   221	3. **Tier-2 loops (H1b, H3).** For three bases, all 32 combinations of the five binary factors (N opaque or physical) give 96 configurations, each run for ten loops (two optimizers, five seeds) at $B=100$, so each main effect compares 480 against 480 loops. Each configuration also gets a 300-edit profile, whose forecast is frozen before loops are scored, and three extra 100-evaluation loops whose pooled per-task development counts give HGM's estimate from as many evaluations (their token and CPU costs are reported).
   222	4. **Parent selection (H5).** One hundred nodes from tier-2 archives, where behavior varies, each get five continuation loops of 20 evaluations. The first feeds the estimators at 0, 5 and 20 descendants; the other four measure empirical metaproductivity as the mean selection gain of each loop's best descendant. As a secondary test, uniform, DGM, HGM, HGM-Dual and HGM with the prior run on the three reserved bases with ten seeds at $B=150$.
   223	5. **Refactor first (H6).** Three bases, each started from the as-written form and from cell 1.
   224	    - *Arms:* $C_R=0$, 10 and 25 with the gate, and 25 without, each crossed with two optimizers and five seeds and run to 200 evaluation-equivalents (240 loops).
   225	    - *Prediction:* $B^{\star}$ from 300-edit development-success profiles of the six starting forms and of the twelve refactored variants from each gated arm's first seed (5,400 edits).
   226	    - *Crossover:* in the Meta-Harness-style loop, each checkpoint's incumbent (every 25 evaluation-equivalents) is fixed by development error alone and then scored on selection people. The crossover is the first checkpoint after which the refactor arm stays below search alone, compared with the prediction rounded to the same grid.
   227	
   228	![Tier 1 crosses a fractional factorial with bases and proposers, and tier 2 runs full loops on 96 configurations](images/05/two-tier-design.svg)
   229	
   230	*Figure 8. Tier 1 (top) profiles every cell for 20 bases and three proposers, with naming and control arms on cells 1 and 8 (dashed). Tier 2 (bottom) correlates, within base, two predictors built from 300 evaluations each with the sealed-test gain of ten loops per configuration.*
   231	
   232	### Ablations and controls
   233	
   234	Each control rules out one explanation; all but the last run with every proposer on 20 bases at 30 edits per variant.
   235	
   236	- **Length.** Padded cell 1 is the length-matched placebo for cell 8, and unpadded copies of cells 1 and 8 measure what padding itself does.
   237	- **Vocabulary.** A synonym-renamed cell 8 separates changed names from more informative ones.
   238	- **Trace source.** An external tracer gives cell 1 O's per-stage summaries while its code stays as written; if this matches the O main effect, the emitting harness adds nothing beyond the information. Because the check uses one cell, O effects are reported as the joint effect of instrumented code and its trace.
   239	- **Trace format.** A raw-trace cell 8 receives its per-trial trace files in place of the summary under the same token budget, separating a null for O from a null for aggregated O.
   240	- **Label noise.** Cells 1 and 8 are rerun with the 30B proposer and development labels permuted across people. Any fall in permuted-label development error is fitting noise, so a larger fall for cell 8 means the richer trace helps the loop fit noise. A selection advantage that survives permutation comes from label-free repairs, such as finding an empty reach window.
   241	
   242	The optimizer may not edit the evaluator or the checks, and no proposer can have seen the unpublished StaBLE bases or the new coding variants in pretraining.
   243	
   244	### Statistical analysis
   245	
   246	The primary model is a logistic mixed model on tier-1 edits,
   247	
   248	$$\mathrm{logit}~P(Z_e=1)=\beta_0+\sum_f\beta_f c_{fe}+\gamma_{q(e)}+\sum_f\theta_{f,q(e)}c_{fe}+u^{base}_{b(e)}+u^{cell}_{b(e)c(e)}+u^{sess}_{k(e)},$$
   249	
   250	with $c_{fe}\in\lbrace -1,1\rbrace$ the level of factor $f$ for edit $e$, proposer effects $\gamma_q$, factor-by-proposer interactions $\theta_{f,q}$, and random intercepts for base, base by cell, and session. Effects are average marginal effects (the mean change in predicted success probability when a factor moves from degraded to structured), with bootstrap intervals over bases, sessions and selection people. The smallest effect of interest is 0.05 (0.10 against 0.15), and the equivalence margin is 0.025. Secondary main-effect tests use Holm correction across five contrasts: the four factorial main effects and physical against generic naming, pooled over cells 1 and 8.
   251	
   252	Power comes from a preliminary simulation and closed-form checks. Edits in a session are correlated, inflating variance by the design effect $1+(m-1)\rho$ for session size $m$ and intraclass correlation $\rho$; at $\rho=0.05$, ten-edit sessions give 1.45, and detecting 0.10 against 0.15 at 80% power takes about 995 edits per arm. Twenty bases give power 0.99, 0.95 and 0.72 for base-by-arm heterogeneity of 0, 0.2 and 0.4 on the logit scale, and three bases at most 0.38.
   253	
   254	For H1, 1,200 edits per cell give a standard error of 0.016 and two-sided power 0.87 (0.81 at heterogeneity 0.2). A TOST with margin 0.025 passes only if the estimate lies within 0.025 minus 1.645 standard errors of zero, so at the default 2,400 edits per factor level (standard error 0.0104) a true null passes with probability 0.55 per factor and 0.09 for all four. The frontier proposer's 5,600 edits per level give 0.0068, per-factor power 0.96 (0.92 at heterogeneity 0.2) and joint power 0.84 (0.71). Equivalence is therefore claimed factor by factor, and a blanket null for the frontier proposer needs all four.
   255	
   256	With loops paired by base, the minimum detectable effect (MDE) at 80% power is
   257	
   258	$$\mathrm{MDE}=2.80\,\sigma_{loop}\sqrt{2/n_{loop}}$$
   259	
   260	for seed-to-seed spread $\sigma_{loop}$ of sealed-test gain and $n_{loop}$ loops per arm. Tier-2 main effects (H1b) detect $0.18\sigma_{loop}$, below the smallest effect of interest, $0.25\sigma_{loop}$; refactor and parent-selection arms of 30 loops detect only $0.72\sigma_{loop}$, so H5's primary test uses archive nodes. For H3, 96 configurations detect a positive correlation with power near 1. The comparison with the clade estimate uses one statistic, the mean over bases of the within-base difference between the two correlations, with a bootstrap that resamples sealed people jointly across configurations and seeds within configuration. Simulated with independent configurations, its power is 0.87 when the true correlations are 0.7 and 0.3 but 0.38 at 0.6 and 0.4; shared people lower both, so it is powered only for gaps above 0.4.
   261	
   262	Mediation (H4) uses the interventional approach of Imai and colleagues with a sensitivity analysis [25](#ref-25), since mediators are not randomized, and repair times use a discrete-time hazard model censored at 30. For H7 the minimum detectable difference between the domains' O effects is about 0.054, above the effect of interest, so H7 is exploratory.
   263	
   264	### Second domain: a self-improving coding agent
   265	
   266	The replication uses Aider Polyglot, 225 Exercism problems in six languages [26](#ref-26), with the DGM [6](#ref-6) and SICA [8](#ref-8) seed agents as bases. Problems are split by exercise name (100 development, 50 selection, 75 sealed), so an exercise in several languages stays in one split. The 30B model is both the frozen task model and the proposer. A $2\times2$ design over M and O plus an opaque-naming arm gives five length-matched variants per base with 250 edits each, so each main effect compares 1,000 against 1,000 edits. An edit succeeds when the selection pass count rises by more than the 95th percentile of rerun-to-rerun changes of the unchanged parent. Parent selection is replicated with HGM's 800-evaluation protocol [7](#ref-7) for DGM, HGM, HGM-Dual and HGM with a refit prior, with five seeds each.
   267	
   268	## How to read the results
   269	
   270	| Outcome | What it would mean | What we would do next |
   271	|---|---|---|
   272	| H1, H1b and H3 hold | Code has a measurable improvability that score hides | Release the profile tool and prior |
   273	| H1 and H1b fail, and frontier main effects pass TOST | Structure does not matter at these budgets, against AHE's premise | Publish the equivalence result |
   274	| Padded cell 1 matches cell 8, or the external trace matches O | Length or delivered information drives the effect | Study length or feedback directly |
   275	| Raw-trace cell 8 beats cell 8 while O is null | Aggregation limits what the trace delivers | Repeat O with raw traces |
   276	| Cell 8 lowers permuted-label development error more than cell 1 | The richer trace helps the loop fit noise | Audit for development-set leakage |
   277	| Profile correlation below 0.6, or no better than the clade estimate | One-step and long-run improvability diverge | Profile two-edit chains |
   278	| Prior no better than HGM's at 0 and 5 descendants | Code features carry no metaproductivity signal | Drop the prior, keep the profile |
   279	| Refactoring first never wins, or misses its crossover | The loop model is too crude, or refactors do not raise $sg$ | Add diminishing returns |
   280	
   281	## Compute, data and scope
   282	
   283	The 30B model is assumed to process 5,000 tokens per second on one H100, and the 480B model 2,500 per second on an eight-H100 node. A tier-1 edit uses about 16,500 tokens (15,000 input, mostly padded code and trace, and 1,500 output) and a loop step about 22,000. Tool CPU is bounded by the pilot's 1.5 s per example, and a task family has about 300 development and 150 selection examples, so an edit costs at most 11 CPU-minutes and a loop step 7.4. Scoring an accepted candidate on selection people costs 3.75, budgeted for 40% of the 219,700 loop steps below.
   284	
   285	| Item | Units | Tokens | H100-hours | CPU-hours |
   286	|---|---|---|---|---|
   287	| Cache OpenCap Monocular outputs (if needed) | 2,968 clips at 3.8 min | none | 188 | small |
   288	| Tier 1, 30B proposer | 12,600 edits | 208M | 12 | 2,310 |
   289	| Tier 1, 480B proposer | 10,200 edits | 168M | 150 | 1,870 |
   290	| Tier 1, frontier API proposer | 16,600 edits | 274M | none | 3,040 |
   291	| Seeded-defect probe | 14,400 steps | 317M | 18 | 1,780 |
   292	| Tier-2 loops | 96,000 steps | 2.1B | 117 | 11,840 |
   293	| Tier-2 profiles | 28,800 edits | 475M | 26 | 5,280 |
   294	| Tier-2 clade-estimate loops | 28,800 steps | 634M | 35 | 3,550 |
   295	| Archive nodes | 10,000 steps | 220M | 12 | 1,230 |
   296	| Parent selection | 22,500 steps | 495M | 28 | 2,780 |
   297	| Refactor first | 48,000 steps | 1.06B | 59 | 5,500 |
   298	| Refactor profiles and gates | 5,400 edits, 2,100 gated refactors | 89M | 5 | 1,380 |
   299	| Selection scoring of accepted candidates | 87,900 candidates | none | none | 5,490 |
   300	| Second domain | 2,500 edits on 50 problems each, plus replication | 4.5B | 250 | small |
   301	
   302	For example, tier-2 loops are 96,000 steps (96 configurations, ten loops, 100 steps); at 22,000 tokens each that is 2.1 billion tokens, or 422,000 seconds at 5,000 tokens per second, or 117 H100-hours. A 200-step pilot run checks these token counts first. The second-domain row assumes 30,000 tokens per Polyglot attempt, so scoring 2,500 edits on 50 problems each takes about 3.8 billion tokens, and reruns, the replication and sealed scoring add 0.7 billion.
   303	
   304	The total is about 710 H100-hours on Stanford HAIC (900 if OpenCap Monocular must be rerun), 10.3 billion open-model tokens and 46,100 CPU-hours. The frontier proposer's 274 million tokens cost about 370 to 1,120 USD at assumed prices of 1 to 3 USD per million input and 5 to 15 USD per million output tokens. StaBLE videos require lab access; features, scores and code are public [1](#ref-1), [3](#ref-3), as are Aider Polyglot and both seed agents. No model is trained.
   305	
   306	## Risks and limitations
   307	
   308	- **Transformations could carry hints.** A variant flagged by the audit is dropped from every arm of its base.
   309	- **Successes could be shrinkage.** Mild shrinkage that keeps ranks and over half the spread passes the guard, so guard failures and Spearman correlations are reported beside every success rate.
   310	- **Bases are a sample.** Claims cover harnesses written like these 20; tier-2 claims are conditional on three bases and the 30B proposer.
   311	- **The reference is video-derived.** StaBLE labels come from three-camera OpenCap, which limits clinical claims more than this question about approaching a fixed target.
   312	- **The sealed people are not new.** They were scored once in the pilot, the only cohort available, so a confirmatory claim would need a fresh cohort.
   313	- **The clade event is adapted.** HGM counts solved tasks, so H5 in biomechanics tests an adaptation to per-task error improvements, and the coding replication tests HGM's own counts.
   314	
   315	## Where to learn more
   316	
   317	1. **Stanford CS329Z, Assignment 1, "Building an Agent Harness"** ([repository](https://github.com/cs329z/assignment1-harness)): builds the kind of harness rewritten here.
   318	2. **CS329Z, Lecture 9, "Optimization"** ([slides](https://web.stanford.edu/class/cs329z/slides/lecture09.pdf)): GEPA and MIPRO, two prompt optimizers that read traces.
   319	3. **Stanford CS329A, Mon Oct 13, "Open-Ended Evolution of Self-Improving Agents"** ([schedule](https://cs329a.stanford.edu/#schedule)): the DGM archive and its parent selection.
